<p style="color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem;">
By <a href="/en/urban-gavelin/" rel="author">Urban Gavelin</a>, <time datetime="2026-10-26">October 26, 2026</time>, AI Productivity &amp; Sales Leadership
</p>
<h1>The Time You Free Up With AI Turns Into Sand</h1>
<img src={coverImage} alt="The Time You Free Up With AI Turns Into Sand" width="1280" height="720" class="hero-img" />
<p class="lead"><strong>Receipts were the dullest task in my business. Not hard, just tedious. It took about five minutes each time, and now it takes none at all, which sounds like very little but is the whole point this week.</strong></p>

<div class="section">
<h2>Time Disappears When No One Decides What It's For</h2>
<p>I automated receipts in September. Now I share the photo from my phone and it lands with the accounting firm on its own. Five minutes saved per receipt. Do that often enough and it adds up to a full working day over a year.</p><p>The day itself isn't the interesting part. What's interesting is where it goes. I talked about this over lunch in August, and one sentence has stayed with me since: efficiency is worthless if the time it frees up just gets filled with more work.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>On My Own Team, I Decided in Advance</h2>
<p>For me it's simple. I run my business fifteen to twenty hours a week, deliberately, and the time goes to things I'd rather do. I decided that in advance.</p><p>On a sales team, almost no one has decided that. You roll out a tool that writes the meeting notes, fills in the CRM, and drafts the follow-up email. Each rep gets back a chunk of time every day. No one notices where it goes. One more internal meeting, a few extra emails, a report someone has always wanted. The time fills up, because time without a name always does.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>Six Months Later, Nobody Can Say Where It Went</h2>
<p>Half a year later, the CFO asks what the investment delivered. Reps don't feel less stressed. The number of customer meetings is the same. The conclusion becomes that AI didn't work for you.</p><p>It worked fine. The time just leaked out.</p><p>There's an old observation that work expands to fill the time available for it. It holds just as true on a sales team as in a government office. Free time doesn't stay free for long. If you don't have a plan for it, someone else's plan fills the gap instead, and that plan rarely involves customers.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>What to Do on Monday</h2>
<p>This is also why I think the question is bigger than it looks. The idea behind growing without hiring is that the same people get more done. Freed-up time is that capacity. Let it leak and you've paid for the tool, skipped the hire, and still haven't gotten more done. Freed-up time is a leadership question, not a technology question. Someone has to decide what it becomes before the tool is switched on, and someone has to check that it did.</p><ul><li>Pick one task that should get shorter. Time it today with a stopwatch, not a guess.</li><li>Decide before you automate where the time should go. Write it down in one line.</li><li>Put the time on the calendar as a recurring block with a name. Time without a name becomes meetings.</li><li>Count what the time should produce, before and after: prepared customer meetings, followed-up lost deals, or whatever you chose.</li><li>Check back after a month. Don't ask if you saved time. Ask what became of it.</li></ul><p>If every rep on your team got back a full day each month starting tomorrow, who has already decided what that day is for?</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<div itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
<h3 itemprop="name">Why does freed-up time disappear even when a tool clearly saves minutes?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<p itemprop="text">Because time without an assigned purpose gets absorbed by whatever is nearby, an extra meeting, a few more emails, a report nobody asked for. Unless someone names what the time is for before the tool goes live, it fills up on its own.</p>
</div>
</div>

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
<h3 itemprop="name">How can a sales leader stop AI-driven time savings from being wasted?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<p itemprop="text">Decide in advance what the freed-up time should produce, block it on the calendar with a clear name, and measure the outcome after a month instead of asking whether time was saved. That turns a vague efficiency gain into a tracked business result.</p>
</div>
</div>

